---
import InsertLayout from '../../layouts/InsertLayout.astro';
import { KIT_OFFERS, KIT_ON_SALE } from '../../config/kit';
import '../../styles/kit.css';
const cases = [
  ['101', 'Matched', '$10.00 and 1,000 cents agree after the declared conversion.'],
  ['102', 'Missing', 'The source row has no target row and no declared rejection.'],
  ['103', 'Changed', '$30.00 became 3,200 cents. That is a different value.'],
  ['104', 'Rejected but present', 'The review said to exclude this row. It still arrived.'],
  ['105', 'Unverified', 'Both balances are blank. This example treats that as unknown.'],
  ['106', 'Ambiguous target', 'Two target rows share this key. Neither is chosen silently.']
];
---
<InsertLayout title="A migration report you can inspect before buying" heading="Six rows in. Six rows out. Five still need a decision." subheading="A fictional acceptance packet, generated by the Migration Acceptance Kit." description="Inspect a real generated migration report, findings and fictional CSV inputs. Equal row counts conceal missing rows, changed values and duplicate keys. No signup or upload.">
  <p class="kit-kicker">Free evaluation sample · actual tool output · fictional data</p>
  <p>A client migration has reached sign-off. Both exports contain six rows. That sounds reassuring until you compare the keys and values. This example shows the evidence you can hand to the person making the acceptance decision.</p>
  <p><a class="kit-buy" href="/kit/sample/output/report.html" data-astro-reload>Open the complete sample report</a></p>
  <p class="kit-buy-terms">No account, email address or upload. The report is a self-contained HTML file you can save and inspect offline. Publication adds a security policy; the generated report content is unchanged.</p>

  <h2>What the six rows actually say</h2>
  <div class="kit-table-wrap"><table class="kit-table"><caption>Fictional source rows, reconciled against the target</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">Result</th><th scope="col">Decision to investigate</th></tr></thead><tbody>{cases.map(([key, result, reason]) => <tr><th scope="row">{key}</th><td>{result}</td><td>{reason}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
  <p>The source accounts for one row in each of these six categories. The target accounts for four attributable rows and two duplicate-key rows. Both totals equal six. The duplicate finding is evidence about the same key on the other side, not another source row.</p>
  <p><strong>The run exits with code 1 under <code>--strict</code>.</strong> A report was produced, but this comparison is not clean. Equal row counts did not make it safe to accept.</p>

  <h2>Inspect the evidence, not just this explanation</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/kit/sample/source.csv" download data-astro-reload>Source CSV</a> and <a href="/kit/sample/target.csv" download data-astro-reload>target CSV</a>: six fictional rows each.</li>
    <li><a href="/kit/sample/rejects.csv" download data-astro-reload>Declared rejection</a> and <a href="/kit/sample/sample-spec.json" download data-astro-reload>this example’s mapping</a>: the decisions the comparison was told to apply.</li>
    <li><a href="/kit/sample/output/findings.csv" download data-astro-reload>Findings CSV</a>: the fix list with source/target side, bucket, key, line and detail.</li>
    <li><a href="/kit/sample/output/report.json" download data-astro-reload>Report JSON</a>: input fingerprints, complete accounting and a reproducible result hash.</li>
    <li><a href="/kit/sample/evaluation-packet.zip" download data-astro-reload>Download the whole evaluation packet</a>, including an interpretation note. This contains sample data and outputs; the paid tool’s source is not included.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="kit-hash">Result content hash: d6e0d47a686e6ad49102aa0338286a66162844dd26ed756c1f44b6542e9c145f</p>

  <h2>Where the review still needs you</h2>
  <p>This example compares keys and one balance field. It does not inspect a live database, prove that an export is complete, check every business rule or approve a migration. The exclusion and blank-value policy are fictional choices—not recommendations for your data.</p>
  <p>Before accepting real work, agree the scope and exceptions, investigate findings, rerun the comparison after changes, and keep the unresolved scope visible in the sign-off. The <a href="/kit/acceptance-checklist/">complete free acceptance checklist</a> explains that process.</p>

  <h2>Use the kit on your own migration</h2>
  <p>The paid kit supplies the Python tool, tests, procedure and sign-off template. It runs locally with the Python standard library. You prepare the exports and declare the mapping; no database credentials or customer data are uploaded to us.</p>
  <div class="kit-offers">{KIT_OFFERS.map(offer => <section class="kit-offer"><h3>{offer.name}</h3><p class="kit-price">US${offer.price}</p><p>{offer.who}</p><ul>{offer.grants.map(grant => <li>{grant}</li>)}</ul>{KIT_ON_SALE ? <a class="kit-buy" href={offer.paymentLink} rel="nofollow">Buy this licence — US${offer.price}</a> : <p>Purchasing is currently unavailable.</p>}</section>)}</div>
  <p>One payment, perpetual licence, no automatic renewal. Consultancy buyers can use the kit on paid client work and hand clients the outputs; the kit’s source stays with the licensee. Read the <a href="/kit/license/">complete licence and 30-day refund terms</a> and <a href="/kit/#price">product and delivery details</a> before deciding.</p>
  <p>Questions about purchase or access: <a href="mailto:hello@toledotechnologies.com">hello@toledotechnologies.com</a>. The existing licence’s contact and rights are unchanged.</p>
  <p class="kit-kicker">Made by Toledo Technologies. Explanation prepared with AI assistance; sample output generated and checked with the shipped tool.</p>
</InsertLayout>
